.TH mail "" "Command"
.SH NAME
mail \- computer mail
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmail [\-mpqr] [\-f \fIfile\fB] [\fIuser ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
mail -- computer mail
Usage:	mail [ options ] [ user ... ]
Options:
	-f file		Print mail from 'file' instead of the default
	-m		Notify each logged in recipient when mail is sent
	-p		Print mail non-interactively
	-q		Exit on interrupt, leaving mail unchanged
	-r		Print mail in reverse order
If 'user' is present, send each a mail message read from standard input.
Mail message ends with EOF or a line containing only '.'.
Otherwise, read commands interactively, as follows:
	d		Delete current message; print the next
	m [user ...]	Mail current message to each 'user'
	p		Print this message again
	q		Quit and update mailbox
	s [file ...]	Save current message with header in each 'file'
	t [user ...]	Send message from standard input to each 'user'
	w [file ...]	Write current message without header in each 'file'
	x		Exit without updating mailbox
	newline		Print the next message
	-		Print the previous message
	EOF		Quit and update mailbox;  same as 'q' command
	?		Print a command summary
	!command	Pass 'command' to the shell to be executed
.HE
.B mail
is the COHERENT electronic postal system.
Invoked with one or more
.I user
arguments,
.B mail
reads a message from the standard input and sends a copy to each
named
.IR user ;
the message ends at end of file or at a line containing only a
single period.
Invoked with no
.I user
argument,
.B mail
opens the invoking user's mailbox and lets the user read, delete,
save, forward, and reply to the messages in it.
.PP
.B mail
recognizes the following options:
.IP \fB\-f\ \fIfile\fR
Read mail from
.I file
instead of the default mailbox.
.IP \fB\-m\fR
When sending mail, notify each recipient who is currently logged in
by writing a message to that user's terminal.
.IP \fB\-p\fR
Print the mailbox non-interactively: print every undeleted message
and exit, without prompting for commands.
.IP \fB\-q\fR
Exit immediately on an interrupt, leaving the mailbox unchanged.
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Print messages in reverse order, latest first.
.PP
When reading a mailbox interactively,
.B mail
prints each undeleted message in turn and prompts
.RB ( ? )
for a command.
.DS
.ta 1.2i
	\fBd\fR	Delete the current message; print the next message
	\fBm\fR [user ...]	Mail the current message to each named user
	\fBp\fR	Print the current message again
	\fBq\fR	Quit, updating the mailbox
	\fBs\fR [file ...]	Save the message, with its header, in each file
	\fBt\fR [user ...]	Mail the standard input to each named user
	\fBw\fR [file ...]	Save the message, without its header, in each file
	\fBx\fR	Exit without updating the mailbox
	newline, \fB+\fR	Print the next message
	\fB\-\fR	Print the previous message
	EOF	Put undeleted mail back and quit; same as \fBq\fR
	\fB?\fR	Print this command summary
	\fB!\fIcommand\fR	Pass \fIcommand\fR to the shell to execute
.DE
.PP
If no
.I file
is given to
.B s
or
.BR w ,
.B mail
saves the message in
.B mbox
in the user's home directory.
If no
.I user
is given to
.B m
or
.BR t ,
.B mail
sends to the invoking user.
When a message cannot be delivered to every named recipient,
.B mail
saves it in
.B dead.letter
in the sender's home directory.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 1.75i
	\fB/usr/spool/mail/\fIuser\fR	incoming mailbox for user
	\fB$HOME/mbox\fR	default file for saved messages
	\fB$HOME/dead.letter\fR	undeliverable outgoing mail
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B sh
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B mail
complains and exits if it cannot open the mailbox, if the mailbox
does not have mailbox format, or if a named recipient has no entry
in the password file.
.SH NOTES
.B mail
is written to run set-user-id to
.BR root ,
checking every file access itself against the invoking user's
permissions.
.B mail
does not implement mail to a remote system in the form
.IB system !user;
attempting to send to such an address prints an error and the
message is not delivered.
.PP
This command's source file is
.B 7mail.c
in the source tree (an unused alternate implementation exists as
.BR xmail.c ),
but it is built and installed as
.BR mail .
